  urldate = {2017-05-16},
  abstract = {Summary Partition coefficients (K's) between dimyristoyl lecithin liposomes and water were measured as a function of temperature for 16 nonelectrolytes. FromK and its temperature dependence, the partial molar free energy (?F), enthalpy (?H) and entropy (?S) of partition for each solute were extracted. By subtraction of ?F, ?H and ?S of hydration of each solute (where known), ?F, ?H and ?S of solution in lecithin could be calculated for some solutes. Among different solutes ?S of solution increases approximately linearly with ?H of solution, as also found in bulk solvents (the so-called Barclay-Butler relations). The Barclay-Butler sloped?S/d?H is approximately twice as steep for lecithin as for bulk nonpolar solvents. This effect is attributed in part to greater immobilization of solutes in bilayers than in bulk solvents.},
