  urldate = {2025-01-03},
  abstract = {Version 1.2.2 Release Notes (July 14, 2023) New features: add {$<$}code{$>$}ModelResult.uvars{$<$}/code{$>$} output to a {$<$}code{$>$}ModelResult{$<$}/code{$>$} after a successful fit that contains {$<$}code{$>$}ufloats{$<$}/code{$>$} from the {$<$}code{$>$}uncertainties{$<$}/code{$>$} package which can be used for downstream calculations that propagate the uncertainties (and correlations) of the variable Parameters. (PR \#888) Outputs of residual functions, including {$<$}code{$>$}Model.\_residual{$<$}/code{$>$}, are more explicitly coerced to 1d-arrays of datatype Float64. This decreases the expectation for the user-supplied code to return ndarrays, and increases the tolerance for more "array-like" objects or ndarrays that are not Float64 or 1-dimensional. (PR \#899) {$<$}code{$>$}Model.fit{$<$}/code{$>$} now takes a {$<$}code{$>$}coerce\_farray{$<$}/code{$>$} option, defaulting to {$<$}code{$>$}True{$<$}/code{$>$} to control whether to input data and independent variables that are "array-like" are coerced to ndarrays of datatype Float64 or Complex128. If set to {$<$}code{$>$}False{$<$}/code{$>$} then independent data that "array-like" ({$<$}code{$>$}pandas.Series{$<$}/code{$>$}, int32 arrays, etc) will be sent to the model function unaltered. The user may then use other features of these objects, but may also need to explicitly coerce the datatype of the result the change described above about coercing the result causes problems. (Discussion \#873; PR \#899) Bug fixes/enhancements: fixed bug in {$<$}code{$>$}Model.make\_params(){$<$}/code{$>$} for non-composite models that use a prefix (Discussion \#892; Issue \#893; PR \#895) fixed bug with aborted fits for several methods having incorrect or invalid fit statistics. (Discussion \#894; Issue \#896; PR \#897) {$<$}code{$>$}Model.eval\_uncertainty{$<$}/code{$>$} now correctly calculates complex (real/imaginary pairs) uncertainties for Models that generate complex results. (Issue \#900; PR \#901) {$<$}code{$>$}Model.eval{$<$}/code{$>$} now returns and array-like value. This adds to the coercion features above and fixes a bug for composite models that return lists (Issue \#875; PR \#901) the HTML representation for a {$<$}code{$>$}ModelResult{$<$}/code{$>$} or {$<$}code{$>$}MinimizerResult{$<$}/code{$>$} are improved, and create fewer entries in the Table of Contents for Jupyter lab. (Issue \#884; PR \#883; PR \#902)},
